# `prompt-unmarked` at a function-typed parameter (Jimmy, 14 November, ruling PT, choice
# (a)): `@Use` gives back what applying `Make` gives, in that tick, so its result is prompt
# in `Make` and the parameter must say so. Without the mark, a caller's lambda capturing an
# event of the caller would smuggle that event through `@Use` unseen (a soundness hole:
# neg/prompt-cycle-through-captured-event).
# Expected text derived by hand; never run.
# Written by Claude on the claude-self-hosting branch; subject to change.
emit Ui.*

@Use: fn (fn !Nat -> @!Nat) -> @!Nat
fn @Use Make:
    !made <- Make never
    @ !made
